{\bkmkend AAAAAAACBW}
{
\pard\plain \s61\li360\sa60\sb30\qj\widctlpar\qj\adjustright \fs20\cgrid 
\par
{
{\s17\sa60\sb30\widctlpar\qj \fs22\cgrid 
Defines how the Nth texture of a specific type is combined with the result of all previous layers. }}\par
{\s17\sa60\sb30\widctlpar\qj \fs22\cgrid 
Example (left: key, right: value): \par
 {
\par
\pard\plain \s41\li360\widctlpar\adjustright \shading1000\cbpat8 \f2\fs16\cgrid DiffColor0     - gray\par
DiffTextureOp0 - aiTextureOpMultiply\par
DiffTexture0   - tex1.png\par
DiffTextureOp0 - aiTextureOpAdd\par
DiffTexture1   - tex2.png\par
}
 Written as equation, the final diffuse term for a specific pixel would be: {
\par
\pard\plain \s41\li360\widctlpar\adjustright \shading1000\cbpat8 \f2\fs16\cgrid diffFinal = DiffColor0 * sampleTex(DiffTexture0,UV0) +\par
   sampleTex(DiffTexture1,UV0) * diffContrib;\par
}
 where 'diffContrib' is the intensity of the incoming light for that pixel. \par
}{\par
{\s5\sb90\sa30\keepn\widctlpar\adjustright \b\f1\fs16\cgrid 
Enumerator:\par}
\pard\plain \s81\li360\widctlpar\ql\adjustright \fs20\cgrid \trowd \trgaph108\trleft426\tblind426\trbrdrt\brdrs\brdrw10\brdrcf15 \trbrdrl\brdrs\brdrw10\brdrcf15 \trbrdrb\brdrs\brdrw10\brdrcf15 \trbrdrr\brdrs\brdrw10\brdrcf15 \trbrdrh\brdrs\brdrw10\brdrcf15 \trbrdrv\brdrs\brdrw10\brdrcf15 
\clvertalt\clbrdrt\brdrs\brdrw10\brdrcf15 \clbrdrl\brdrs\brdrw10\brdrcf15 \clbrdrb\brdrs\brdrw10\brdrcf15 \clbrdrr \brdrs\brdrw10\brdrcf15 \cltxlrtb \cellx2187
\clvertalt\clbrdrt\brdrs\brdrw10\brdrcf15 \clbrdrl\brdrs\brdrw10\brdrcf15 \clbrdrb\brdrs\brdrw10\brdrcf15 \clbrdrr \brdrs\brdrw10\brdrcf15 \cltxlrtb \cellx8748
\pard \widctlpar\intbl\adjustright
{\xe \v aiTextureOp_Multiply\:material.h}
{\xe \v material.h\:aiTextureOp_Multiply}
{\qr aiTextureOp_Multiply{\bkmkstart AAAAAAACBX}
{\bkmkend AAAAAAACBX}
\cell }{{\s17\sa60\sb30\widctlpar\qj \fs22\cgrid 
T = T1 * T2 \par
}\cell }{\row }
{\xe \v aiTextureOp_Add\:material.h}
{\xe \v material.h\:aiTextureOp_Add}
{\qr aiTextureOp_Add{\bkmkstart AAAAAAACBY}
{\bkmkend AAAAAAACBY}
\cell }{{\s17\sa60\sb30\widctlpar\qj \fs22\cgrid 
T = T1 + T2 \par
}\cell }{\row }
{\xe \v aiTextureOp_Subtract\:material.h}
{\xe \v material.h\:aiTextureOp_Subtract}
{\qr aiTextureOp_Subtract{\bkmkstart AAAAAAACBZ}
{\bkmkend AAAAAAACBZ}
\cell }{{\s17\sa60\sb30\widctlpar\qj \fs22\cgrid 
T = T1 - T2 \par
}\cell }{\row }
{\xe \v aiTextureOp_Divide\:material.h}
{\xe \v material.h\:aiTextureOp_Divide}
{\qr aiTextureOp_Divide{\bkmkstart AAAAAAACCA}
{\bkmkend AAAAAAACCA}
\cell }{{\s17\sa60\sb30\widctlpar\qj \fs22\cgrid 
T = T1 / T2 \par
}\cell }{\row }
{\xe \v aiTextureOp_SmoothAdd\:material.h}
{\xe \v material.h\:aiTextureOp_SmoothAdd}
{\qr aiTextureOp_SmoothAdd{\bkmkstart AAAAAAACCB}
{\bkmkend AAAAAAACCB}
\cell }{{\s17\sa60\sb30\widctlpar\qj \fs22\cgrid 
T = (T1 + T2) - (T1 * T2) \par
}\cell }{\row }
{\xe \v aiTextureOp_SignedAdd\:material.h}
{\xe \v material.h\:aiTextureOp_SignedAdd}
{\qr aiTextureOp_SignedAdd{\bkmkstart AAAAAAACCC}
{\bkmkend AAAAAAACCC}
\cell }{{\s17\sa60\sb30\widctlpar\qj \fs22\cgrid 
T = T1 + (T2-0.5) \par
}\cell }{\row }
{\xe \v _aiTextureOp_Force32Bit\:material.h}
{\xe \v material.h\:_aiTextureOp_Force32Bit}
{\qr _aiTextureOp_Force32Bit{\bkmkstart AAAAAAACCD}
{\bkmkend AAAAAAACCD}
\cell }{\cell }{\row }
}
}
{\xe \v aiTextureType\:material.h}
{\xe \v material.h\:aiTextureType}
\pard\plain \s4\sb240\sa60\keepn\widctlpar\adjustright \b\f1\fs20\cgrid {
{\b 
{\b enum} {\b aiTextureType}}}
\par
{\bkmkstart AAAAAAACCE}
{\bkmkend AAAAAAACCE}
{
\pard\plain \s61\li360\sa60\sb30\qj\widctlpar\qj\adjustright \fs20\cgrid 
\par
{
{\s17\sa60\sb30\widctlpar\qj \fs22\cgrid 
Defines the purpose of a texture. }}\par
{\s17\sa60\sb30\widctlpar\qj \fs22\cgrid 
This is a very difficult topic. Different 3D packages support different kinds of textures. For very common texture types, such as bumpmaps, the rendering results depend on implementation details in the rendering pipelines of these applications. {\b Assimp} loads all texture references from the model file and tries to determine which of the predefined texture types below is the best choice to match the original use of the texture as closely as possible.\par
In content pipelines you'll usually define how textures have to be handled, and the artists working on models have to conform to this specification, regardless which 3D tool they're using. \par
}{\par
{\s5\sb90\sa30\keepn\widctlpar\adjustright \b\f1\fs16\cgrid 
Enumerator:\par}
\pard\plain \s81\li360\widctlpar\ql\adjustright \fs20\cgrid \trowd \trgaph108\trleft426\tblind426\trbrdrt\brdrs\brdrw10\brdrcf15 \trbrdrl\brdrs\brdrw10\brdrcf15 \trbrdrb\brdrs\brdrw10\brdrcf15 \trbrdrr\brdrs\brdrw10\brdrcf15 \trbrdrh\brdrs\brdrw10\brdrcf15 \trbrdrv\brdrs\brdrw10\brdrcf15 
\clvertalt\clbrdrt\brdrs\brdrw10\brdrcf15 \clbrdrl\brdrs\brdrw10\brdrcf15 \clbrdrb\brdrs\brdrw10\brdrcf15 \clbrdrr \brdrs\brdrw10\brdrcf15 \cltxlrtb \cellx2187
\clvertalt\clbrdrt\brdrs\brdrw10\brdrcf15 \clbrdrl\brdrs\brdrw10\brdrcf15 \clbrdrb\brdrs\brdrw10\brdrcf15 \clbrdrr \brdrs\brdrw10\brdrcf15 \cltxlrtb \cellx8748
\pard \widctlpar\intbl\adjustright
{\xe \v aiTextureType_NONE\:material.h}
{\xe \v material.h\:aiTextureType_NONE}
{\qr aiTextureType_NONE{\bkmkstart AAAAAAACCF}
{\bkmkend AAAAAAACCF}
\cell }{{\s17\sa60\sb30\widctlpar\qj \fs22\cgrid 
Dummy value.\par
No texture, but the value to be used as 'texture semantic' (#aiMaterialProperty::mSemantic) for all material properties {\i not}  related to textures. \par
}\cell }{\row }
{\xe \v aiTextureType_DIFFUSE\:material.h}
{\xe \v material.h\:aiTextureType_DIFFUSE}
{\qr aiTextureType_DIFFUSE{\bkmkstart AAAAAAACCG}
{\bkmkend AAAAAAACCG}
\cell }{{\s17\sa60\sb30\widctlpar\qj \fs22\cgrid 
LEGACY API MATERIALS Legacy refers to materials which Were originally implemented in the specifications around 2000. These must never be removed, as most engines support them. The texture is combined with the result of the diffuse lighting equation. OR PBR Specular/Glossiness \par
}\cell }{\row }
{\xe \v aiTextureType_SPECULAR\:material.h}
{\xe \v material.h\:aiTextureType_SPECULAR}
{\qr aiTextureType_SPECULAR{\bkmkstart AAAAAAACCH}
{\bkmkend AAAAAAACCH}
\cell }{{\s17\sa60\sb30\widctlpar\qj \fs22\cgrid 
The texture is combined with the result of the specular lighting equation. OR PBR Specular/Glossiness \par
}\cell }{\row }
{\xe \v aiTextureType_AMBIENT\:material.h}
{\xe \v material.h\:aiTextureType_AMBIENT}
{\qr aiTextureType_AMBIENT{\bkmkstart AAAAAAACCI}
{\bkmkend AAAAAAACCI}
\cell }{{\s17\sa60\sb30\widctlpar\qj \fs22\cgrid 
The texture is combined with the result of the ambient lighting equation. \par
}\cell }{\row }
{\xe \v aiTextureType_EMISSIVE\:material.h}
{\xe \v material.h\:aiTextureType_EMISSIVE}
{\qr aiTextureType_EMISSIVE{\bkmkstart AAAAAAACCJ}
{\bkmkend AAAAAAACCJ}
\cell }{{\s17\sa60\sb30\widctlpar\qj \fs22\cgrid 
The texture is added to the result of the lighting calculation. It isn't influenced by incoming light. \par
}\cell }{\row }
{\xe \v aiTextureType_HEIGHT\:material.h}
{\xe \v material.h\:aiTextureType_HEIGHT}
{\qr aiTextureType_HEIGHT{\bkmkstart AAAAAAACCK}
{\bkmkend AAAAAAACCK}
\cell }{{\s17\sa60\sb30\widctlpar\qj \fs22\cgrid 
The texture is a height map.\par
By convention, higher gray-scale values stand for higher elevations from the base height. \par
}\cell }{\row }
{\xe \v aiTextureType_NORMALS\:material.h}
{\xe \v material.h\:aiTextureType_NORMALS}
{\qr aiTextureType_NORMALS{\bkmkstart AAAAAAACCL}
{\bkmkend AAAAAAACCL}
\cell }{{\s17\sa60\sb30\widctlpar\qj \fs22\cgrid 
The texture is a (tangent space) normal-map.\par
Again, there are several conventions for tangent-space normal maps. {\b Assimp} does (intentionally) not distinguish here. \par
}\cell }{\row }
{\xe \v aiTextureType_SHININESS\:material.h}
{\xe \v material.h\:aiTextureType_SHININESS}
{\qr aiTextureType_SHININESS{\bkmkstart AAAAAAACCM}
{\bkmkend AAAAAAACCM}
\cell }{{\s17\sa60\sb30\widctlpar\qj \fs22\cgrid 
The texture defines the glossiness of the material.\par
The glossiness is in fact the exponent of the specular (phong) lighting equation. Usually there is a conversion function defined to map the linear color values in the texture to a suitable exponent. Have fun. \par
}\cell }{\row }
{\xe \v aiTextureType_OPACITY\:material.h}
{\xe \v material.h\:aiTextureType_OPACITY}
{\qr aiTextureType_OPACITY{\bkmkstart AAAAAAACCN}
{\bkmkend AAAAAAACCN}
\cell }{{\s17\sa60\sb30\widctlpar\qj \fs22\cgrid 
The texture defines per-pixel opacity.\par
Usually 'white' means opaque and 'black' means 'transparency'. Or quite the opposite. Have fun. \par
}\cell }{\row }
{\xe \v aiTextureType_DISPLACEMENT\:material.h}
{\xe \v material.h\:aiTextureType_DISPLACEMENT}
{\qr aiTextureType_DISPLACEMENT{\bkmkstart AAAAAAACCO}
{\bkmkend AAAAAAACCO}
\cell }{{\s17\sa60\sb30\widctlpar\qj \fs22\cgrid 
Displacement texture\par
The exact purpose and format is application-dependent. Higher color values stand for higher vertex displacements. \par
}\cell }{\row }
{\xe \v aiTextureType_LIGHTMAP\:material.h}
{\xe \v material.h\:aiTextureType_LIGHTMAP}
{\qr aiTextureType_LIGHTMAP{\bkmkstart AAAAAAACCP}
{\bkmkend AAAAAAACCP}
\cell }{{\s17\sa60\sb30\widctlpar\qj \fs22\cgrid 
Lightmap texture (aka Ambient Occlusion)\par
Both 'Lightmaps' and dedicated 'ambient occlusion maps' are covered by this material property. The texture contains a scaling value for the final color value of a pixel. Its intensity is not affected by incoming light. \par
}\cell }{\row }
{\xe \v aiTextureType_REFLECTION\:material.h}
{\xe \v material.h\:aiTextureType_REFLECTION}
{\qr aiTextureType_REFLECTION{\bkmkstart AAAAAAACCQ}
{\bkmkend AAAAAAACCQ}
\cell }{{\s17\sa60\sb30\widctlpar\qj \fs22\cgrid 
Reflection texture\par
Contains the color of a perfect mirror reflection. Rarely used, almost never for real-time applications. \par
}\cell }{\row }
{\xe \v aiTextureType_BASE_COLOR\:material.h}
{\xe \v material.h\:aiTextureType_BASE_COLOR}
{\qr aiTextureType_BASE_COLOR{\bkmkstart AAAAAAACCR}
{\bkmkend AAAAAAACCR}
\cell }{{\s17\sa60\sb30\widctlpar\qj \fs22\cgrid 
PBR Materials PBR definitions from maya and other modelling packages now use this standard. This was originally introduced around 2012. Support for this is in game engines like Godot, Unreal or Unity3D. Modelling packages which use this are very common now. \par
}\cell }{\row }
{\xe \v aiTextureType_NORMAL_CAMERA\:material.h}
{\xe \v material.h\:aiTextureType_NORMAL_CAMERA}
{\qr aiTextureType_NORMAL_CAMERA{\bkmkstart AAAAAAACCS}
{\bkmkend AAAAAAACCS}
\cell }{\cell }{\row }
{\xe \v aiTextureType_EMISSION_COLOR\:material.h}
{\xe \v material.h\:aiTextureType_EMISSION_COLOR}
{\qr aiTextureType_EMISSION_COLOR{\bkmkstart AAAAAAACCT}
{\bkmkend AAAAAAACCT}
\cell }{\cell }{\row }
{\xe \v aiTextureType_METALNESS\:material.h}
{\xe \v material.h\:aiTextureType_METALNESS}
{\qr aiTextureType_METALNESS{\bkmkstart AAAAAAACCU}
{\bkmkend AAAAAAACCU}
\cell }{\cell }{\row }
{\xe \v aiTextureType_DIFFUSE_ROUGHNESS\:material.h}
{\xe \v material.h\:aiTextureType_DIFFUSE_ROUGHNESS}
{\qr aiTextureType_DIFFUSE_ROUGHNESS{\bkmkstart AAAAAAACCV}
{\bkmkend AAAAAAACCV}
\cell }{\cell }{\row }
{\xe \v aiTextureType_AMBIENT_OCCLUSION\:material.h}
{\xe \v material.h\:aiTextureType_AMBIENT_OCCLUSION}
{\qr aiTextureType_AMBIENT_OCCLUSION{\bkmkstart AAAAAAACCW}
{\bkmkend AAAAAAACCW}
\cell }{\cell }{\row }
{\xe \v aiTextureType_SHEEN\:material.h}
{\xe \v material.h\:aiTextureType_SHEEN}
{\qr aiTextureType_SHEEN{\bkmkstart AAAAAAACCX}
{\bkmkend AAAAAAACCX}
\cell }{{\s17\sa60\sb30\widctlpar\qj \fs22\cgrid 
PBR {\b Material} Modifiers Some modern renderers have further PBR modifiers that may be overlaid on top of the 'base' PBR materials for additional realism. These use multiple texture maps, so only the base type is directly defined Sheen Generally used to simulate textiles that are covered in a layer of microfibers eg velvet {\f2 https://github.com/KhronosGroup/glTF/tree/master/extensions/2.0/Khronos/KHR_materials_sheen} \par
}\cell }{\row }
{\xe \v aiTextureType_CLEARCOAT\:material.h}
{\xe \v material.h\:aiTextureType_CLEARCOAT}
{\qr aiTextureType_CLEARCOAT{\bkmkstart AAAAAAACCY}
{\bkmkend AAAAAAACCY}
\cell }{{\s17\sa60\sb30\widctlpar\qj \fs22\cgrid 
Clearcoat Simulates a layer of 'polish' or 'laquer' layered on top of a PBR substrate {\f2 https://autodesk.github.io/standard-surface/#closures/coating} {\f2 https://github.com/KhronosGroup/glTF/tree/master/extensions/2.0/Khronos/KHR_materials_clearcoat} \par
}\cell }{\row }
{\xe \v aiTextureType_TRANSMISSION\:material.h}
{\xe \v material.h\:aiTextureType_TRANSMISSION}
{\qr aiTextureType_TRANSMISSION{\bkmkstart AAAAAAACCZ}
{\bkmkend AAAAAAACCZ}
\cell }{{\s17\sa60\sb30\widctlpar\qj \fs22\cgrid 
Transmission Simulates transmission through the surface May include further information such as wall thickness \par
}\cell }{\row }
{\xe \v aiTextureType_UNKNOWN\:material.h}
{\xe \v material.h\:aiTextureType_UNKNOWN}
{\qr aiTextureType_UNKNOWN{\bkmkstart AAAAAAACDA}
{\bkmkend AAAAAAACDA}
\cell }{{\s17\sa60\sb30\widctlpar\qj \fs22\cgrid 
Unknown texture\par
A texture reference that does not match any of the definitions above is considered to be 'unknown'. It is still imported, but is excluded from any further post-processing. \par
}\cell }{\row }
